  9: 
 10: > **Hosted version for Claude.ai, ChatGPT, and Grok coming soon.** [**Join the waitlist →**](https://tally.so/r/A7WVge?ref=cronometer-api-mcp)
 11: 
 12: An [MCP (Model Context Protocol)](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/) server for [Cronometer](https://cronometer.com/) nutrition tracking, built on the reverse-engineered mobile REST API.
 13: 
 14: Unlike [cronometer-mcp](https://github.com/cphoskins/cronometer-mcp), which takes a comprehensive GWT-RPC approach against Cronometer's web backend, this server talks to the same JSON REST API used by the Cronometer Android app -- with clean payloads and stable, versioned endpoints.
 15: 
 16: ## Features
 17: 
 18: - **Food log** -- diary entries with food names, amounts, meal groups
 19: - **Nutrition data** -- daily macro/micro totals and nutrition scores with per-nutrient confidence
 20: - **Food search** -- search the Cronometer food database, get detailed nutrition info
 21: - **Diary management** -- add/remove entries, copy days, mark days complete
 22: - **Custom foods** -- create foods with custom nutrition data
 23: - **Macro targets** -- read weekly schedule and saved templates
 24: - **Fasting** -- view history and aggregate statistics
 25: 
 26: ## Quick Start
 27: 
 28: ### 1. Install [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
 29: 
 30: ```bash
 31: curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
 32: ```
 33: 
 34: ### 2. Set credentials
 35: 
 36: ```bash
 37: export CRONOMETER_USERNAME="your@email.com"
 38: export CRONOMETER_PASSWORD="your-password"
 39: ```
 40: 
 41: ### 3. Configure your MCP client
 42: 
 43: `uvx` downloads and runs the server on demand -- no separate install step.
 44: 
 45: #### OpenCode (`opencode.json`)
 46: 
 47: ```json
 48: {
 49:   "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
 50:   "mcp": {
 51:     "cronometer": {
 52:       "type": "local",
 53:       "command": ["uvx", "cronometer-api-mcp"],
 54:       "environment": {
 55:         "CRONOMETER_USERNAME": "{env:CRONOMETER_USERNAME}",
 56:         "CRONOMETER_PASSWORD": "{env:CRONOMETER_PASSWORD}"
 57:       },
 58:       "enabled": true
 59:     }
 60:   }
 61: }
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: #### Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`)
 65: 
 66: ```json
 67: {
 68:   "mcpServers": {
 69:     "cronometer": {
 70:       "command": "uvx",
 71:       "args": ["cronometer-api-mcp"],
 72:       "env": {
 73:         "CRONOMETER_USERNAME": "your@email.com",
 74:         "CRONOMETER_PASSWORD": "your-password"
 75:       }
 76:     }
 77:   }
 78: }
 79: ```
 80: 
 81: ## Available Tools
 82: 
 83: ### Food Log & Nutrition
 84: 
 85: | Tool | Description |
 86: |------|-------------|
 87: | `get_food_log` | Diary entries for a date with food names, amounts, and meal groups, plus an energy_summary (target/consumed/remaining kcal) |
 88: | `get_daily_nutrition` | Daily macro and micronutrient totals |
 89: | `get_nutrition_scores` | Category scores (Vitamins, Minerals, etc.) with per-nutrient consumed amounts and confidence levels |
 90: 
 91: ### Food Search & Details
 92: 
 93: | Tool | Description |
 94: |------|-------------|
 95: | `search_foods` | Search the Cronometer food database by name |
 96: | `get_food_details` | Full nutrition profile and serving sizes for a food |
 97: 
 98: ### Diary Management
 99: 
100: | Tool | Description |
101: |------|-------------|
102: | `add_food_entry` | Log a food serving to the diary |
103: | `remove_food_entry` | Remove one or more diary entries |
104: | `add_custom_food` | Create a custom food with specified nutrition |
105: | `copy_day` | Copy all entries from the previous day |
106: | `mark_day_complete` | Mark a diary day as complete or incomplete |
107: 
108: ### Targets & Tracking
109: 
110: | Tool | Description |
111: |------|-------------|
112: | `get_macro_targets` | Weekly macro schedule and saved target templates |
113: | `get_fasting_history` | Fasting history within a date range |
114: | `get_fasting_stats` | Aggregate fasting statistics |
115: 
116: All date parameters use `YYYY-MM-DD` format and default to today when omitted.
117: 
118: ## Remote Deployment
119: 
120: The server supports remote deployment with OAuth 2.1 authorization (PKCE) for use with Claude.ai and other remote MCP clients.
121: 
122: ### Environment Variables
123: 
124: | Variable | Required | Description |
125: |----------|----------|-------------|
126: | `CRONOMETER_USERNAME` | Yes | Cronometer account email |
127: | `CRONOMETER_PASSWORD` | Yes | Cronometer account password |
128: | `MCP_TRANSPORT` | No | Transport mode: `stdio` (default), `sse`, or `streamable-http` |
129: | `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN` | Remote | Bearer token gating the MCP endpoint (header only). **Required** for `sse`/`streamable-http`. |
130: | `MCP_APPROVE_SECRET` | Remote | Secret entered once in the browser approve page. **Required** for `sse`/`streamable-http` (min 16 chars). Keep distinct from `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN`. |
131: | `MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` | No | OAuth client ID for remote clients |
132: | `MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` | No | OAuth client secret for remote clients |
133: | `MCP_BASE_URL` | No | Public base URL for OAuth metadata endpoints (should be `https://`) |
134: | `PORT` | No | Listen port for remote transports (default 8000) |
135: 
136: > **Remote transports fail closed.** When `MCP_TRANSPORT` is `sse` or `streamable-http`, the server refuses to start unless both `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN` and `MCP_APPROVE_SECRET` are set. The approve page is gated by `MCP_APPROVE_SECRET` (with per-IP rate limiting and a 5-minute auth-code TTL) so the OAuth flow cannot be used by an anonymous caller to mint the bearer token.
137: 
138: ### Dokku / Heroku Deployment
139: 
140: The project includes a `Procfile` and `.python-version` for direct deployment with the Heroku Python buildpack:
141: 
142: ```bash
143: # Create app
144: dokku apps:create cronometer-api-mcp
145: 
146: # Set environment
147: dokku config:set cronometer-api-mcp \
148:   MCP_TRANSPORT=streamable-http \
149:   MCP_AUTH_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32) \
150:   MCP_APPROVE_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32) \
151:   MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=my-client \
152:   MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32) \
153:   MCP_BASE_URL=https://your-domain.com \
154:   CRONOMETER_USERNAME=your@email.com \
155:   CRONOMETER_PASSWORD=your-password
156: 
157: # Deploy
158: git push dokku main
159: ```
160: 
161: ### Claude.ai Remote Connection
162: 
163: When deployed remotely with OAuth configured, connect from Claude.ai using:
164: 
165: - **Server URL**: `https://your-domain.com/mcp`
166: - **OAuth Client ID**: Value of `MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID`
167: - **OAuth Client Secret**: Value of `MCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`
168: 
169: Claude.ai will open a browser tab for authorization. The page prompts for the **access secret** -- enter the value of `MCP_APPROVE_SECRET` to complete the connection. You do this once on first connect (and again on any later re-auth).
170: 
171: ### ⚠️ Docker security
172: 
173: The published Docker image wraps the server with supergateway in **stdio** mode. In stdio mode the OAuth approve-secret gate is **not** applied -- the image exposes every tool with no authentication. Do **not** expose the Docker container directly to the public internet.
174: 
175: For authenticated remote access, deploy using the native transport (`MCP_TRANSPORT=streamable-http` with `MCP_AUTH_TOKEN` and `MCP_APPROVE_SECRET` set), which engages the built-in OAuth gate. If you must run the Docker image with public exposure, place it behind an authenticating reverse proxy (Caddy basicauth, Cloudflare Access, Tailscale, etc.).
176: 
177: ## Development
178: 
179: For local development, copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in your credentials:
180: 
181: ```bash
182: cp .env.example .env
183: # edit .env
184: uv run cronometer-api-mcp
185: ```
186: 
187: The CLI auto-loads `.env` on startup (dev convenience only). Real environment variables always win over `.env`, so production deployments and MCP client `env` blocks are unaffected.
188: 
189: ## How It Works
190: 
191: This server communicates with `mobile.cronometer.com` -- the same REST API used by the Cronometer Android/Flutter app. The API was reverse-engineered through:
192: 
193: 1. Static analysis of `libapp.so` (Dart AOT snapshot) from the APK to discover endpoint names
194: 2. Traffic interception via Frida + mitmproxy to capture exact request/response formats
195: 3. Trial-and-error against the live API to confirm payload shapes
196: 
197: The API uses two protocols:
198: 
199: - **v2 (`POST /api/v2/*`)** -- JSON-body auth, used for most operations (food search, diary read/write, nutrition, fasting, macros)
200: - **v3 (`DELETE /api/v3/user/{id}/*`)** -- Header-based auth (`x-crono-session`), used for diary entry deletion
201: 
202: ## Python API
203: 
204: You can use the client directly:
205: 
206: ```python
207: from cronometer_api_mcp.client import CronometerClient
208: from datetime import date
209: 
210: client = CronometerClient()
211: 
212: # Search for foods
213: results = client.search_food("chicken breast")
214: 
215: # Get food details
216: food = client.get_food(results[0]["id"])
217: 
218: # Log a serving
219: client.add_serving(
220:     food_id=food["id"],
221:     measure_id=food["defaultMeasureId"],
222:     grams=200,
223: )
224: 
225: # Get today's diary
226: diary = client.get_diary()
227: 
228: # Get nutrition scores
229: scores = client.get_nutrition_scores()
230: ```
